International Human Rights Courts
The International Court Of Justice (ICJ)
What Is the International Court of Justice?
The International Court of Justice (ICJ), also known as the World Court, is the principal judicial
organ of the United Nations, established in 1945 with the adoption of the UN Charter. Based in The
Hague, Netherlands, the ICJ is the only court of a universal character with the purpose of settling
legal disputes between states and providing advisory opinions on international legal matters
referred to it by the UN.
What Does The ICJ Do?
The primary aim of the ICJ is to promote the peaceful resolution of international disputes and
contribute to the development of international law. The ICJ operates through two primary functions:
settling disputes between states and issuing advisory opinions on legal questions.
● Settling Disputes: When states bring cases before the ICJ, the Court’s role is to adjudicate
based on international law. Only states can be parties in cases before the ICJ, meaning that
individuals and organisations cannot submit cases directly.
○ Once the Court reaches a decision, its judgments are binding, and the states
involved are expected to comply, although enforcement can sometimes be difficult
as state sovereignty means the ICJ can’t enforce the rulings.
● Advisory Opinions: The ICJ also issues advisory opinions at the request of the UN
General Assembly, Security Council, or other UN agencies. These opinions are non-binding
but carry significant legal and moral weight, helping clarify international law and guide the
actions of the UN and its member states.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 7
How Much Influences Does The ICJ Have?
Arguments That The ICJ Has Limited Influence:
The ICJ’s effectiveness is limited by the fact that states must consent to its jurisdiction for disputes
to be heard and for rulings to be enforced. If a state refuses to recognise the Court’s authority or
declines to implement its rulings, there are few mechanisms to compel compliance. Russia, China
and the United States among others don’t permanently recognise the ICJ’s jurisdiction, so the ICJ
can only rule in a case involving them if they consent to it on a case by case basis.
Additionally, advisory opinions issued by the ICJ are non-binding, meaning that states can ignore
them without facing legal consequences. This undermines the Court’s ability to act as a decisive
force in resolving international conflicts or holding states accountable for violations of international
human rights law.
Finally, the ICJ is also limited as it only deals with cases between states and does not have
jurisdiction over individuals. This limits its direct impact on holding perpetrators of human rights
abuses accountable. For such cases, bodies like the International Criminal Court (ICC) are more
effective in prosecuting individuals responsible for severe crimes such as genocide and war
crimes.
South Africa v. Israel - Example Of The ICJ’s Ability To Draw Attention To Human Rights
Abuses But Failure To Enforce Its Decisions:
● After South Africa brought a case against Israel in the ICJ in late 2023, accusing it of
committing genocide against Palestinians in Gaza, the ICJ issued a ruling in early 2024
ordering Israel to prevent genocidal acts and let humanitarian aid into Gaza.
○ In May 2024, the ICJ went even further and ordered Israel to immediately halt its
military offensive in Rafah.
● On the one hand, this is a good example of how the ICJ can hold states to account for
human rights abuses, by allowing states to pursue cases against other states and draw
attention to human rights violations that breach international law.
● On the other hand, it is also a clear example of the ICJ’s limited enforcement power.
Although its rulings are legally binding in theory, the ICJ relies on state compliance for
enforcement, as the UN doesn’t have its own police force.
○ In this case, Israel rejected the ICJ’s jurisdiction and continued its war in Gaza.
