The International Criminal Court (ICC)
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 9
What Is the International Criminal Court?
The International Criminal Court (ICC) is an independent judicial body established to prosecute
individuals for the most serious offenses of concern to the international community, including
genocide, war crimes, crimes against humanity, and crimes of aggression. Created through the
Rome Statute in 1998 and operational since 2002, the ICC is headquartered in The Hague,
Netherlands.
Unlike the International Court of Justice (ICJ), which handles disputes between states, the ICC
focuses specifically on individual criminal accountability, making it a unique mechanism for
enforcing human rights on a global scale.
What Does the ICC Do?
The ICC’s mission is to end impunity for perpetrators of human rights abuses and promote justice,
thus contributing to international peace and security. The ICC has jurisdiction over crimes
committed in member states or by nationals of member states and can also accept cases referred
by the UN Security Council.
● Investigations and Prosecutions: The ICC can initiate investigations based on referrals
from member states, the UN Security Council, or at the request of its Prosecutor with
judicial approval.
○ Cases can involve high-profile government officials, military leaders, and others who
have committed grave violations of human rights.
● Victim Participation and Reparations: One notable feature of the ICC is its emphasis on
victim participation. Victims can contribute their views and concerns during proceedings and
may receive reparations following a conviction.
○ This approach underscores the ICC’s commitment to justice not only for the
international community but for those directly affected by human rights abuses.
How Much Influence Does the ICC Have?
Arguments That the ICC Has Limited Influence:
1. It depends on the cooperation of member states to execute arrest warrants and
facilitate investigations. Without this, apprehending suspects can be challenging.
○ For instance, despite being convicted Omar al-Bashir, the former President of
Sudan, avoided arrest for years despite an ICC indictment for crimes related to the
Darfur conflict, as several states including South Africa, Kenya and Jordan refused
to enforce the warrant when he was visiting their countries.
2. 70 states have not signed the Rome Statute, including the United States, China and
Russia, as they don’t want their government officials/leaders to be prosecuted for human
rights abuses.
○ This means that the ICC doesn’t have jurisdiction over these countries and can’t act
against individuals from these nations, demonstrating how the enforcement of
international law is dependent on state compliance.
■ As Russia, China and the U.S. are also members of the UNSC, it means that
the UNSC is very unlikely to recommend prosecutions against them or any
of their close allies.
○ This undermines the court’s universal mandate and creates a perception of selective
justice, particularly against the world’s weaker states. It has also meant that the ICC
has been able to act on a very small number of cases.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 10
3. The ICC has disproportionately targeted leaders from weaker or developing
countries, especially within Africa, despite human rights abuses occuring globally.
○ Up until 2023, all 31 individuals publicly indicted by the ICC were from African
nations, including Uganda, the Democratic Republic of Congo, Sudan, the Central
African Republic, Kenya, Libya, Côte d’Ivoire, and Mali.
○ This resulted in many African Union states threatening to withdraw from the ICC due
to allegations of bias.
Arguments That the ICC Has Significant Influence:
1. The ICC allows perpetrators of war crimes to be directly held accountable, which
acts as a significant deterrent against future violations.
○ The knowledge that individuals, regardless of their political power, could be
prosecuted and held personally liable for human rights violations acts as a deterrent
and helps reinforce adherence to international human rights law.
2. The ICC has demonstrated its effectiveness through successful convictions and
punishments of individuals who have committed war crimes.
○ The ICC has secured convictions against 10 individuals, all of whom have been
sentenced to imprisonment.
○ Many of these are serving these sentences, including Thomas Lubunga who was
convicted in 2012 for recruiting child soldiers and Dominic Ongwen who was
convicted in 2021 for crimes including murder and sexual enslavement as former
commander of Uganda’s Lord’s Resistance Army.
3. In 2023 and 2024, the ICC demonstrated it doesn’t just pursue cases in Africa.
○ The ICC issued an arrest warrant for Russian President Vladimir Putin and for
alleged war crimes related to the conflict in Ukraine. It was able to as Ukraine,
where the alleged crimes took place, has signed the Rome Statute.
○ Additionally, the ICC has issued arrest warrants for Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin
Netanyahu and former Defense Minister Yoav Gallant, as well as Hamas leaders
Yahya Sinwar, Mohammad Deif, and Ismail Haniyeh, for alleged war crimes and
crimes against humanity in Gaza.
■ It was able to do so as the Palestinian Territories have signed the Rome
Statute.
Case Studies Of The ICC
Case Study - The ICC and China’s Uyghur Genocide
The ICC has faced significant limitations in investigating alleged genocide and crimes against
humanity by China against the Uyghur Muslim population in Xinjiang. Despite reports of mass
detentions and severe human rights abuses, the ICC has been unable to open a formal
investigation because China is not a member of the Rome Statute.
Without China’s cooperation or acknowledgment of the ICC’s jurisdiction, the Court cannot
proceed. This case illustrates how the ICC’s jurisdictional limitations and reliance on state
membership hinder its ability to address potential human rights violations by powerful
non-member states and uphold international human rights law.
Case Study - Arrest Warrants Against Insraeli and Hamas Leaders
In November 2024, the ICC issued arrest warrants for Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu
and former Defence Minister Yoav Gallant, as well as for Hamas leaders Yahya Sinwar,
Mohammad Deif, and Ismail Haniyeh. The charges relate to alleged war crimes and crimes
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 11
against humanity committed in Gaza since October 2023, including the use of starvation as a
method of warfare and attacks on civilians by the Israeli military, and hostage-taking by Hamas.
This was the first time the ICC had issued arrest warrants for Israeli politicians or Hamas leaders
and led to criticism from the Israeli government. However, ICC Prosecutor Karim Khan stated
there was sufficient evidence to pursue these charges under international law. Even though Israel
hasn’t ratified the Rome Statute, the ICC could pursue the charges as the Palestinian territories
have and it therefore has jurisdiction.
This supports the view that the ICC is an effective human rights court, as it has demonstrated a
willingness and ability to investigate alleged war crimes by leaders outside of Africa, even though
Israel is a close ally of the U.S., the world’s most powerful state.
This being said, Israel’s government has refused to accept the ruling and none of the defendants
have been arrested, showing how the ICC’s effectiveness is limited by the fact it relies on
member states to execute its arrest warrants.
Case Study - The Conviction Of Thomas Lubanga
In 2012, the ICC secured its first-ever conviction in the case against Thomas Lubanga, a former
leader of the Union of Congolese Patriots in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Lubanga was
found guilty of war crimes, specifically for enlisting and conscripting child soldiers and using them
actively in hostilities. Lubanga’s conviction demonstrated the Court’s ability to bring justice to
victims and reinforced the message that perpetrators of war crimes, regardless of their position,
could be held accountable. He was sentenced to 14 years in prison, which he served, showing
the ICC’s capacity not only to secure convictions but also to ensure the enforcement of
sentences.
Case Study - The Arrest Of Former President of the Philippines Rodrigo Duterte
In March 2025, former Philippine President Rodrigo Duterte was arrested in Manilla and
transferred to The Hague to face charges at the International Criminal Court (ICC) for crimes
against humanity. The charges relate to his administration’s brutal ‘War on Drugs’ during which
thousands of alleged drug dealers and users were killed without trial between 2016 and 2019.
Though Duterte had withdrawn the Philippines from the Rome Statute as President in 2019, the
ICC still has jurisdiction over crimes committed by Duterte when the Philippines was still a
member state and his arrest was made possible due to the current President of the Philippines
cooperating with the ICC and handing Duterte over.
This is therefore a good example of the effectiveness of the ICC at holding high ranking officials
and politicians to account for crimes against humanity, including leaders outside of Africa where
the vast majority of previous ICC cases were focused on, leading to allegations of bias.
Case Study - The Conviction Of Malian War Criminal Al Hassan (2025)
In November 2024, former member of the Ansar Dine Islamist group Al Hassan Ag Abdoul Aziz
was found guilty of war crimes and crimes against humanity committed in Timbuktu, Mali
between 2012 and 2013, during the city’s occupation by jihadist forces. The charges included
rape, sexual slavery, persecution, torture, and the destruction of cultural and religious sites. Al
Hassan was head of the Islamic police. He was sentenced to 10 years in prison and
demonstrates how the ICC can be effective in holding political leaders to account for war crimes
that break international law.
