The European Court of Human Rights (ECHR)
What Is the European Court of Human Rights?
The European Court of Human Rights (ECHR), based in Strasbourg, France, is an international
court established to oversee the enforcement of the European Convention on Human Rights. The
ECHR’s primary role is to ensure that member states uphold the civil and political rights enshrined
in the Convention. It has jurisdiction over member states of the Council of Europe, which include
over 46 countries. This includes non-EU member states such as Turket and the United Kingdom.
What Does the ECHR Do?
● Individual Complaints: The Court’s most notable and unique function is that individuals,
non-governmental organisations, or groups of individuals can directly bring cases against
states for alleged violations of their rights under the Convention.
○ This unique feature empowers citizens to hold their governments accountable for
human rights breaches.
● Inter-State Cases: The ECHR can also hear cases brought by one state against another.
While less common, these cases often address significant human rights and international
relations issues.
● Binding Judgments: Decisions made by the ECHR are binding, and member states are
required to comply with the Court’s rulings.
○ The Committee of Ministers of the Council of Europe seeks to ensure the
implementation of the ECHR’s judgments by monitoring states’ compliance, though
it has no real enforcement powers.
How Much Influence Does the ECHR Have?
Arguments That the ECHR Has Limited Influence:
The ECHR’s effectiveness is hampered by non-compliance. While the Court’s rulings are legally
binding, enforcement depends on the political will and cooperation of the member states. Countries
that face repeated findings of violations, such as Russia before its expulsion from the Council of
Europe, demonstrate that non-compliance can persist, undermining the Court’s authority.
Arguments That the ECHR Has Significant Influence:
Despite challenges, the ECHR has played a transformative role in shaping human rights law
across Europe. Its judgments have prompted legal and policy reforms in member states, improving
protections for freedom of expression, fair trial rights, and prohibitions against torture.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 13
The Court’s ability to hear individual complaints ensures a level of accountability that is unique
among international courts, and its decisions have set vital legal precedents influencing both
national and international human rights standards.
Case Studies Of The ECHR
Case Study - M.A. AND Z.R. v. CYPRUS (2024)
In October 2024, the ECHR ruled in favour of two Syrian refugees after they were forcibly
returned to Lebanon by Cypriot authorities without being allowed to apply for asylum. The Court
found that Cyprus had violated Article 3 of the European Convention on Human Rights
(prohibition of inhuman or degrading treatment) and Article 13 (right to an effective remedy),
because it had failed to assess whether the applicants faced a risk of mistreatment once
returned. Cyprus was ordered to pay damages to the pair. This is a good example of how the
ECHR upholds human rights by allowing individuals to bring cases to the court and challenge
states that have infringed upon their human rights.
Case Study - Siliadin v. France (2005)
In Siliadin v. France (2005), the ECHR ruled that France had failed to protect a young Togolese
girl who had been subjected to forced domestic labour and servitude, violating Article 4 of the
European Convention on Human Rights, which prohibits slavery and forced labour. The Court
found that although French criminal law addressed human trafficking, it did not adequately
protect individuals from conditions amounting to servitude. This case was significant in raising
awareness of modern slavery within Europe and pushing member states to strengthen their
legislative frameworks to better combat such abuses. France responded by revising its laws to
provide clearer definitions and stronger protections against forced labour and human trafficking.
This showcased the effectiveness of the ECHR in driving legal reforms and enhancing human
rights safeguards at the national level.
Case Study - Russia’s Non-Compliance with Judgments (Various Cases)
Russia’s non-compliance with ECHR judgments has been a recurring issue, showcasing the
limitations of the Court’s enforcement power. Before its expulsion from the Council of Europe in
2022, Russia routinely ignored ECHR rulings, particularly in cases related to political prisoners,
freedom of expression, and human rights abuses in Chechnya. The state’s consistent refusal to
implement decisions highlighted the limitations of the ECHR’s reliance on the political will of
member states for compliance.
Hirst v. United Kingdom (2005)
In Hirst v. United Kingdom (2005), the ECHR ruled that the UK’s blanket ban on prisoner voting
was a violation of the right to free elections under Article 3 of Protocol No. 1 of the Convention.
Although the judgment emphasised the need for proportionality and legislative reform, the UK
has still failed to introduce voting rights for prisoners. This resistance highlights the challenges
the ECHR faces when domestic political considerations clash with international human rights
obligations. While the case reinforced the ECHR’s ability to identify rights violations, it also
showed the limits of its influence when member states delay or avoid compliance.
