The Impact Of Human Rights Law On State Sovereignty
The relationship between human rights law and state sovereignty is complicated. It undermines
sovereignty more for some states than others and only sometimes needs to do so for it to be
effective. Below are four different perspectives on whether human rights law is undermines state
sovereignty, and whether this means that it is effective or not.
Sovereignty Does Stop Human Rights Law Being Effective, As Human Rights
Law Is Very Dependent on States to Enforce It
● An argument made by realists is that international human rights law doesn’t undermine
state sovereignty and that state sovereignty in fact stops it from being effective.
○ Unlike domestic legal systems that have built-in enforcement mechanisms such as
police forces and judicial oversight, international human rights law depends on
states to accept rulings and implement the suggestions of international courts.
