Case Study – International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia (ICTY)
The International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia (ICTY) was established by UN
Security Council Resolution 827 in 1993 to address serious crimes committed during the Balkan
conflicts of the 1990s. In total, it convicted 90 war criminals. One of its most significant cases
involved Radovan Karadžić, the former President of the Republika Srpska, who was charged with
genocide, war crimes, and crimes against humanity related to the Bosnian War. The ICTY found
Karadžić guilty in 2016, sentencing him to 40 years in prison for orchestrating atrocities, including
the Srebrenica massacre, where over 8,000 Bosniak men and boys were killed.
The ICTY played a pivotal role in holding powerful figures accountable, establishing that
individuals in positions of high authority could face justice for severe human rights abuses. It was
significant in reinforcing international legal standards regarding genocide and command
responsibility. Although the tribunal faced challenges, such as delays in capturing high-profile
suspects like Ratko Mladić and allegations from Serbia that it was biased against Serbs, it
ultimately demonstrated the effectiveness of international mechanisms in prosecuting war crimes
and deterring future violations.
Case Study – International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda (ICTR)
The International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda (ICTR) was created in 1994 under UN Security
Council Resolution 955 to prosecute those responsible for the genocide that claimed
approximately 800,000 lives within 100 days. One of the most notable cases was that of
Jean-Paul Akayesu, a former mayor, who became the first individual to be convicted of genocide
by an international tribunal. The ICTR found him guilty of genocide and crimes against humanity,
including acts of sexual violence, marking a historic moment by establishing that rape could
constitute an act of genocide when used as part of a systematic attack on a group.
This ruling set a precedent in international law and significantly influenced how gender-based
violence is prosecuted in the context of mass atrocities. The ICTR’s work highlighted the
importance of accountability in post-conflict justice and demonstrated the tribunal’s role in
developing international legal standards. While it faced criticisms for slow processes and high
costs, the conviction of figures like Akayesu showcased the tribunal’s effectiveness in addressing
impunity and contributing to the legacy of international criminal justice.
○ As states are primarily motivated by their own interests, they will often refuse to
enforce rulings against themselves. This demonstrates how sovereignty limits the
effectiveness of international human rights law.
● The ICJ, ICC and ECHR can issue ‘binding’ rulings, however they have no real powers to
enforce these rulings if the state doesn’t decide to comply, as the importance of state
sovereignty in the UN and international system means they can’t enter a state to arrest a
war criminal or enforce a decision against a state if that state refuses.
Sovereignty Does Stop Human Rights Law Being Effective, As Powerful
States Can Ignore It
● Another important argument in relation to the impact of human rights law on sovereignty is
that the selective impact of human rights law means that it undermines the sovereignty of
weaker states, whilst powerful states retain their sovereignty and ignore it.
○ Human rights law often proves effective in influencing less powerful states due to
international pressure and the potential repercussions of not accepting the
jurisdiction of international courts or accepting their judgements, including sanctions
or diplomatic isolation.
○ However, more powerful states can ignore these pressures without consequence
and decide not to accept the jurisdiction of international courts or accept their
rulings. As a result, they are able to retain their sovereignty.
● This is seen most clearly in the ICC, where powerful nations such as Russia, China and the
United States haven’t signed the Rome Statute and the ICC therefore can’t prosecute their
leader for war crimes.
○ By contrast, the majority of the world’s poorer and weaker states have been
pressured by liberal states to sign the Rome Statute and all convictions made by the
ICC so far have been against citizens of African states.
● This shows how the liberal ideal that international human rights law protects all citizens
equally is fundamentally flawed.
Human Rights Law Is Sometimes Effective and Undermines State
Sovereignty To Be So
● While there are significant challenges in enforcing human rights law, there are notable
instances where international legal institutions have successfully held states accountable,
thus undermining complete state sovereignty.
○ The liberal perspective argues that this type of enforcement is justified because it
prioritises the protection of human rights over the absolute power of state
sovereignty. Liberals maintain that human rights are universal and must be upheld
even if it means reducing state autonomy.
● International courts such as the ECHR have demonstrated the ability to compel states to
alter their laws and policies in response to rulings.
○ For example, in Goodwin v. United Kingdom (2002), the ECHR ruled that the UK’s
failure to legally recognise a transgender woman’s gender reassignment violated
her rights.
○ The UK subsequently enacted the Gender Recognition Act 2004, illustrating how
international human rights rulings can force sovereign states to change their laws.
● The ICC has also shown its ability to hold political leaders to account for war crimes. For
example, Thomas Lubanga was convicted and imprisoned for human rights violations
includng enlisting child soldiers.
