Human Rights Law Is Sometimes Effective Without Undermining State
Sovereignty
● Another perspective is that human rights law is often effective, but largely due to voluntary
compliance and moral pressure that doesn’t undermine state sovereignty.
○ States often adhere to international human rights norms because doing so aligns
with their values and enhances their global reputation, rather than because they are
forced to by an international court.
○ The liberal perspective supports this view, arguing that international cooperation and
shared norms encourage states to uphold human rights without direct compulsion.
■ This does mean that the effectiveness of human rights law is very dependent
on the willingness of states to act against their interests, though, which
realists would contend is often rare.
● For instance, the ECHR’s judgments often lead to state compliance because of the
reputational benefits of being seen as a protector of human rights. The Court’s ability to
hear individual complaints and make binding judgments can pressure even powerful states
such as the UK into compliance.
